      "text": "Given that \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and we need to find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the relationship between \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{A}\\):**\n   Since \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) is the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix.\n\n2. **Square \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\):**\n   We need to find \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1}\\). First, compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\):**\n   Using the property of inverses:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1}\\):**\n   Since \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{I}\\), the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n   \\[\n   (\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}^{-1}\n   \\]\n   The identity matrix \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) has a well-known inverse:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{I}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final Answer:**\n   Since \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{I}\\), the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n   \\[\n   (\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
