      "text": "To find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we first need to find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). The inverse of a matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is given by \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{\\det(\\mathbf{A})} \\text{adj}(\\mathbf{A})\\), where \\(\\det(\\mathbf{A})\\) is the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) and \\(\\text{adj}(\\mathbf{A})\\) is the adjugate of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\).\n\nGiven \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we recognize that the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) is the same as \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}\\). The inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is given by \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\), because the inverse of a product of matrices is the product of their inverses in reverse order.\n\nLet's calculate \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow, we need to find \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
